al models</article-title><source>Animal Science</source><year>2002</year><volume>75</volume><fpage>15</fpage><lpage>2</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Prentice</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name><name><surname>Gloecker</surname><given-names>LA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Regression analysis of grouped survival data with application to breast cancer</article-title><source>Biometrics</source><year>1978</year><volume>34</volume><fpage>57</fpage><lpage>67</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">630037</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.2307/2529588</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ducrocq</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name></person-group><source>Survival analysis applied to animal breeding and epidemiology</source><publisher-name>Course notes. Wageningen, Pays-Bas</publisher-name><fpage>74</fpage><comment>April 7&#x02013;11 1997</comment></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Reid</surname><given-names>DP</given-names></name><name><surname>Szanto</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Glebe</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Danzmann</surname><given-names>RG</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferguson</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>QTL for body weight and condition factor in Atlantic salmon (<italic>Salmo salar</italic>): comparative analysis with rainbow trout (<italic>Onchorhynhus mykiss</italic>) and Artic charr (<italic>Salvelinus alpinus</italic>)</article-title><source>Heredity</source><year>2005</year><volume>94</volume><fpage>166</fpage><lpage>172</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15483654</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.hdy.6800590</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kittelsen</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Venvik</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Olesen</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Fj&#x000e6;ra</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Nervik</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><source>Drift og r&#x000f8;kt</source><year>2003</year><publisher-name>GAN Forlag, Oslo</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lyttle</surname><given-names>TW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cheaters sometimes prosper: distortion of Mendelian segregation by meiotic drive</article-title><source>Trends Genet</source><year>1993</year><volume>9</volume><fpage>205</fpage><lpage>210</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8337761</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0168-9525(93)90120-7</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gharbi</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Gautier</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Danzmann</surname><given-names>RG</given-names></name><name><surname>Gharbi</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Sakamoto</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>H&#x000f8;yheim</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Taggart</surname><given-names>JB</given-names></name><name><surname>Cairney</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Powell</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Krieg</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Okamoto</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferguson</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name><name><surname>Holm</surname><given